{"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"abstract":[{"text":"The tensor product G × H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy view. Made","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"by ","type":"text"},{"code":"tensorProductView(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Also called the direct, categorical or Kronecker product.","type":"text"}],"relationshipsSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"type":"conformsTo","kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Vertices and their indices are as in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CartesianProductView"},{"type":"text","text":". The row of (g, h) lists "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g′, h′)"},{"type":"text","text":" for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each entry g′ of G’s row of g and each entry h′ of H’s row of h, G outer. Rows are computed from"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the factors’ index rows and never hash."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"An edge’s position is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Position(first: e, second: f, reversed:)"},{"text":": G’s edge ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"text":" and H’s edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"f"},{"text":". In an undirected product two non-loop edges {a, b} and {c, d} give two edges, the straight","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"{(a, c), (b, d)} and the reversed {(a, d), (b, c)}, where a, c are the edges’ ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"u"},{"text":" ends; when","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"either is a self-loop they coincide and there is one, not reversed. A row lists a factor","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop from its first appearance only, so every product edge has each of its ends once in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the rows. Directed, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"reversed"},{"text":" is always false, and the product has edge indices","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·m_H + index(f)"},{"text":" when both factors have them; undirected it has none, because a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop changes how many edges a factor pair gives.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"metadata":{"role":"symbol","title":"TensorProductView","externalID":"s:13GraphProducts17TensorProductViewV","symbolKind":"struct","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-